# Things that must never reach the public mirror because of WHAT THEY ARE, not
# what shape they have: an employer name, a client, an internal project or
# directory. No generic pattern can tell such a name from any other word, and it
# is not a value any config consumes, so the other two halves of
# mise-scripts/no-local-values.sh are blind to it.
#
# Copy to ~/.config/dotfiles/scrub.patterns and fill in:
#
#   install -m 600 -D ~/.local/share/dotfiles/scrub.example.patterns \
#     ~/.config/dotfiles/scrub.patterns
#
# Format:
#   - one POSIX extended regex (grep -E) per line
#   - blank lines and # comments ignored
#   - matched CASE-INSENSITIVELY, so write one spelling, not four
#   - a plain word is a valid line; regex is there when you need variants
#
# The file is untracked and never read by anything but the gate. When a pattern
# fires, only its LINE NUMBER in this file is printed — never the pattern text,
# which would disclose the very string being kept out.
#
# A pattern that does not compile fails the gate loudly rather than being skipped,
# so a typo cannot silently switch a line off.
#
# Keep patterns specific. A short or common word here fails every commit that
# happens to use it in prose; there is no allowlist to climb back out with.

# --- examples, delete these -------------------------------------------------
# Every example below uses the reserved fictional names acme / initech. Do NOT
# illustrate this file with a real identifier: the examples are committed, this
# file is published, and a comment leaks exactly as well as a live pattern.
# acme[- ]?corp
# initech|initech-inc|initechcorp
# ~/Projects/(acme|client-[a-z]+)/
# jdoe@acme\.com
